    "stem": "Reyansh builds an $n$-by-$n$ square of unit tiles. He peels it into $n$ nested L-shaped layers: the outermost L uses $2n-1$ tiles, then $2n-3$, and so on down to $1$.",
    "item_type": "structured",
    "options": {},
    "statements": [],
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": "i",
        "text": "Which claim correctly explains why the sum of the first $n$ odd numbers equals $n^{2}$?",
        "marks": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "The $n$-by-$n$ square partitions into those $n$ nested odd L-layers, so $1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^{2}$."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "Any collection of odd numbers sums to a square, even when the odds are not the first $n$ consecutive odds from $1$."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "The same L-layer story also forces the up-and-down sum of ordinary counting numbers to equal $n^{2}$."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ii",
        "text": "Give one reason for your choice.",
        "marks": 1
      }
    ],
